  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes targeted drug classes and their molecular pathways across four inflammatory diseases: Crohn’s disease, ulcerative colitis, peripheral spondyloarthritis, and axial spondyloarthritis. Each disease is paired with a simple organ or joint drawing and a network of immune targets, showing where available or developing therapies act.\n\nAcross conditions, TNF is a major shared target, with several agents clustered around it. IL-23 and IL-12 also appear repeatedly, especially in the bowel diseases, while the spondyloarthritis panels emphasize additional pathways such as IL-17 receptor signaling. Another recurring theme is intracellular signaling through JAK1, JAK3, TYK2, and PDE4, indicating that some treatments act downstream rather than on extracellular cytokines. In inflammatory bowel disease, integrin α4β7 is highlighted as a gut-specific trafficking target.\n\nThe color coding compares development status: darker blue boxes indicate drugs already approved by the FDA and/or EMA, while lighter blue, yellow, and red mark phase IV, phase III, and phase II clinical trials. Overall, the image conveys that these related immune-mediated diseases share overlapping inflammatory pathways, but the therapeutic landscape differs by disease, with gut disorders showing more integrin-directed treatment and arthritis showing stronger emphasis on IL-17/JAK-related mechanisms.",
